  "id": "DrugBank_DB00047 - MONDO_5015",
  "verbalization": "Insulin Glargine, a long-acting insulin analog, is associated with Diabetes Mellitus (disease) through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Insulin Glargine is linked to Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus via its indication to treat this condition, which in turn is associated with other drugs such as Albiglutide and Gemigliptin. These drugs are indicated for Diabetes Mellitus, indicating a broader pharmacological context in which Insulin Glargine may play a role in disease management. The scores from the paths reflect the strength of these associations, with the highest score (0.8375) linking Insulin Glargine directly to Diabetes Mellitus through a chain involving Insulin Human. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Insulin Glargine and the development or management of Diabetes Mellitus, though the exact mechanisms remain to be fully elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of insulin in the pathophysiology of diabetes, emphasizing the importance of insulin analogs in therapeutic strategies.",
